@ahg-g Yes, those pods exist because if we restart kube-scheduler(change the leader) the new leader is probably checking the status of all of pods, and the Pending pods that might be in this status for 1-2 days get scheduled to suitable nodes.

Unfortunately, there are no specific repro steps, we just run a lot of deployments, most of them get scheduled without any issues(99%), but a few are stuck in Pending status until we either restart the Pods or change kube-scheduler leader.

Regarding  "doesn't exist in informer cache", we haven't seen it before on the previous k8s version (1.18) it appears right after we upgrade to 1.19 and this issue with the Pending pods starts happening.
